<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
    Auto Backup rules for API 30 and BELOW. API 31+ reads data_extraction_rules.xml instead, so
    both files must carry the same exclusion or the gap is silent — minSdk here is 29, so this
    file is live, not legacy.

    https://developer.android.com/guide/topics/data/autobackup
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <!--
        The encrypted token store. Its AES-256/GCM key lives in the Android Keystore and does NOT
        travel with a backup by design, so a restored copy of this file can never be decrypted.
        Backing it up therefore buys nothing and costs two things: a credential-shaped blob in the
        user's cloud, and an undecryptable file that the app reads on every launch after a restore.

        The path is Context.preferencesDataStoreFile(name), i.e. filesDir/datastore/<name>.preferences_pb.
        TokenBackupExclusionTest asserts it against the name constant in :core:network.
    -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/showtrack_tokens.preferences_pb" />

    <!--
        The push registration. `endpoint` is a BEARER SECRET in the same sense the ntfy topic is
        (backend 6-L): whoever holds it can post arbitrary notifications to this device. It is
        withheld by the list endpoint, never written to a log line, and guarded from logcat in
        PushRegistrar — so a cloud backup was the one route by which it left the device, in
        plaintext, while every other surface treated it as a credential.

        A second, quieter reason, independent of secrecy: `targetId` identifies a row on the
        server that belongs to THE DEVICE THAT REGISTERED IT. Restored onto a different phone, the
        next `unregister()` would delete the OLD device's target and silently stop its
        notifications. Neither field survives a restore usefully — the distributor mints a fresh
        endpoint on first run anyway — so excluding the file costs nothing at all.
    -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/showtrack_push.preferences_pb" />
</full-backup-content>
